hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  fear and trembling is a philosophical  work and it deals primarily with faith  understood as trust and as passion not  simply as credence or belief and in it  Kierkegaard is engaging in philosophy so  one of the key questions that is is  raised at one point in the preliminary  expectoration section is what can  philosophies orientation to faith be  what should it be  and by contrast what should it not be  oftentimes being the case in his own  time so he tells us a few things here  that we can piece together into a  full-blown perspective on the  relationship between philosophy and  faith but he doesn't go much further  with this and it's important to keep in  mind that he is writing this as he does  so often in his works under the guise of  a pseudonym in this case it's Johannes  to Silencio who appears to himself be a  philosopher or at least somebody who has  studied philosophy is conversant with  the philosophy of his time and he has  some very definite opinions on that as  well as on faith and theology and you  know what we can say is that Kierkegaard  is really going to give us two sets of  ideas that he's proposing to us  proposing very strongly one is criticism  of contemporary philosophy of his own  time largely Hegelian ISM but it could  extend to many other ways of  understanding philosophy as well so he's  criticizing the attitudes that many  people in his own culture and  intellectual milieu are setting out of  the relationship between philosophy and  faith and then he's elaborating for us a  different model a model in which  philosophy is remaining a bit more true  to its own spirit and mission so let's  look  the criticism first he tells us at a  number of points in this work not just  in this section but but throughout the  entire thing that so many people in his  culture want to you know begin with some  sort of understanding of faith and then  go beyond faith and he also says they  want to do this with the figure of  Abraham the idea is that well you know  Abraham he's got this relationship with  God or the divine or whatever we want to  call it spirit and it's very primitive  it's very crude we don't want to be  beholden to that we want to take  whatever is really essential the lesson  we can learn from it and then get rid of  all that other Stone Age garbage that  that is really just cluttering things up  and when we express it this way I think  you can see that there's not only a lot  of people in Kierkegaard's own  enlightened age who espoused viewpoints  like that there's many people in our own  age as well who view religious faith or  some of its key figures as these  rudimentary starting points that have  some lessons to teach us but we need to  really strip away all the dross and  leave that stuff behind and then call  out the few nuggets of you know whatever  we want to call it perennial philosophy  eternal wisdom you know liberal ideas  for the age pick whatever tagline you  like and Kierkegaard thinks that that's  a really fundamental mistake why would  that be a mistake  well because faith is not something to  begin with as a launching pad and then  leave behind it's actually something  that is very difficult to attain and  that you have to work towards and might  be receding from you yes you're moving  towards it using the means of philosophy  or whatever other intellectual apparatus  you would you would prefer in that place  so having this attitude that  viii is something to go beyond is going  to be quite problematic he's got a  wonderful line in there about Abraham  and about Hegelian philosophy says it's  supposed to be a difficult to understand  Hegel but to understand Abraham is a  small matter now it is difficult to  understand Eagle in part because Hegel  does not express himself well but also  because Hegel is working with quite  complicated concepts and there's this  entire developmental view that's quite  true right but as Abraham dumber than  Hegel is Abraham less expressive is  there less to teach us he says to go  beyond Hegel is a miraculous achievement  but to go beyond Abraham is the easiest  of all and he says I from my own part of  applied considerable time to  understanding a Galilean philosophy I  believe I've understood it fairly well  I'm sufficiently brash to think when I  can't understand particular passages  despite all my pains he himself may not  have been entirely clear this I do  easily naturally without any mental  strain so he's saying I understand the  Hegelian philosophy I may not be you  know the preeminent scholar or  practitioner of it but I get it now what  about Abraham so thinking about Abraham  is an entirely different matter I am  shattered  I am constantly aware of the prodigious  paradox that is the content of Abraham's  life I am constantly repelled and  despite all its passion my thought  cannot penetrate it cannot get ahead by  a hair's breadth I stretch every muscle  to get a perspective and at the very  same instant I become paralyzed now  notice what Kierkegaard is not saying  there he's not saying oh well you know I  didn't understand it the first time or  the second time so it must not be  comprehensible because if it's not  understandable to me as the sort of  representative of what really is or the  adjudicator of what really is  understandable then it must not be  understandable in itself he's saying no  there is a paradox there is  something that resists my rational  efforts to make sense of it before me  and I am trying my hardest to understand  it it's not a fault of the content the  way it might be in hegel hegel being  unclear at points being wrong at points  it's rather me the rational agent trying  to make sense of it who may not be up to  the task so if that's the case then we  probably shouldn't try to you know leap  beyond Abraham or faith in an instant  another example of a criticism that he  makes philosophy he says disparages  faith and give something else in its  place  he says philosophy should not do this  it's not really being faithful to its  own goals its own mission if it's doing  that why should philosophy not disparage  faith we have a lot of philosophical  perspectives that do in fact disparage  faith and not in the sense of saying I  want to understand this faith they say  look this is all garbage these religious  believers they're deluded they are  mistaken they are within the grips of  their own desires and passions they're  projecting something out there that  doesn't really exist here Kierkegaard  doesn't go deeply into this question  here about whether that's a legitimate  position for a philosophy that wants to  remain true to itself as philosophy to  take but he does sort of set out his own  view on this philosophy shouldn't be in  the business of disparaging faith maybe  faith isn't something that philosophy is  really that competent at least in  certain respects to say something about  he doesn't say that by the way that  philosophy shouldn't disparage  superstition or the co-option of  religious beliefs by political systems  or anything like that as a matter of  fact later on in this he will say that a  church is really very much like a state  you know both of them are to be  distrusted but he does think that  philosophy shouldn't be in the business  simply of disparaging faith this  missing it what else  philosophy wants to give something in  place of faith philosophy wants to say  here's what faith really is or here's  the nugget of truth in it here you go  religious believers have this instead  wouldn't you be better off with this  sort of thing then that crude stupid  anthropomorphic thing that you believe  in and practice and Kierkegaard is  saying that you know if philosophy wants  to be honest he says philosophy cannot  and must not give faith you know but it  shouldn't give something else in its  place so then the question is well great  where are you supposed to get faith and  he is going to leave that unanswered at  this point notice one other thing -  before we go into what philosophy ought  to be doing about the conception of  philosophy that he has here and this is  a conception of philosophy that cannot  really be avoided even by those who make  an attempt to avoid it  philosophy is one of those activities of  the human mind that is total izing it  attempts to make sense out in principle  out of everything it attempts to extend  itself to everything it may not in fact  be competent to do so and we're not just  talking about the competency of  particular individual philosophers we're  talking about the competence sort of in  principle of the entire discipline or  activity one of the key questions that  is raised for philosophy by philosophy  itself in relation to other disciplines  is can philosophy go over there into the  domain of other disciplines and tell  them what they ought to be doing or make  full judgments about what they're doing  or fully understand what they're up to  or are there certain limits beyond which  philosophy perhaps either shouldn't  cross or shouldn't cross and expect its  competence to be respected shouldn't  tell itself that it belongs there on  that side of the limit many philosophers  in the past  and even in the present even those who  would like to limit philosophies own  pretensions in certain ways in a  philosophical way often do not respect  that that set of limits that are imposed  by the other objects of human thought  and activity so he tells us that  philosophy should do several things one  is it just a little bit later he's  talking about the dialectic of faith and  he says it's commonly supposed that what  faith produces is no work of art that is  a coarse and boorish piece of work only  for the most uncouth nature's crude  right and then he says but it's far from  being that the dialectic of faith is the  finest and most extraordinary of all it  has an elevation of which I can  certainly form a conception but no more  than that so notice that Kierkegaard  here is not saying faith over there  hands-off philosophy you've got no  business whatsoever  he's saying philosophy can indeed form a  conception of what faith is up to but it  can't do more than that it doesn't  understand it fully it doesn't provide  the blueprint for it faith if it wants  to teach philosophy can teach philosophy  something about itself and about its  dialectic so what should we do we should  form a conception of the dialectic of  faith we should in fact employ  philosophy to do so that is a legitimate  use of that activity that rational  pursuit what else he goes on and he says  that philosophy should indeed understand  itself this is in that passage that I'll  not read he says I by no means conclude  the faith is something inferior but  rather that it's the highest and that  it's dishonest of philosophy to give  something else in its place and to  disparage faith we saw that just a  little bit earlier says philosophy  cannot and must not give faith  but here's a key but at this point it  must understand itself and know what it  offers and take nothing away  least of all trick men out of something  by pretending that it is nothing that  faith is nothing now you might say well  why does philosophy have to understand  itself this again is integral to the  classic conception of philosophy a  philosophy that does not make an attempt  to understand itself its own activities  its own limitations is not really much  of a philosophy it becomes something  like an ideology or like a set of  algorithms or like a set of instructions  about how to do something that has no  reflexive capacity and that would  invalidate it as philosophy if you put  it side by side with philosophy that  actually does try to understand itself  that does attempt to make sense out of  itself that would be a significant  problem  would it not what else he brings up this  this idea of faith and he uses two  different terms here as being higher  than and being beyond what it is that  philosophy can do so philosophy cannot  dominate analyze put something in its  place for faith and it actually has to  recognize that faith is in a certain  sense greater than philosophy and that  is something that philosophy can in fact  do it will be very uncomfortable for  most philosophers to do that but that  does not mean that philosophy cannot or  should not do that  and if we follow Kierkegaard's  conception that he's laying out here  what this means is that philosophy is  always going to be in the position of a  learner or you might say a beginner when  it comes to faith rather than being the  expert talking down  - and giving rules to faith and you  might bring up one other question here  just to bring this to a close is this a  reversion to what we might call the  medieval understanding of philosophy as  the handmaiden of theology the answer is  no because Kierkegaard actually has a  very dim view of the theology of his  time he says that love has its priests  and the poets who speaks to the honor of  the passion of faith he says philosophy  goes further theology sits rouged and  powdered in the window and courts  philosophies favor offers its charms to  philosophy he's saying essentially sort  of reversing Luther's dictum that reason  is a [ __ ] that theology is in fact  acting like a prostitute and taking  faith and putting it out in front of  philosophy in order to sell it which  makes theology something pretty bad so  we don't have you know a simple  subordination of philosophy to theology  instead we might say we have faith as a  kind of limit beyond which philosophy is  drawn but also extends beyond its own  competence and it is philosophical for  philosophy to recognize that so that is  one key idea governing this work and  hopefully that that will help you  understand the approach Kierkegaard is  taking  you
 

